        "## **Pattern analysis & Key Findings**\n",
        "\n",
        "**1) Money Matters:**\n",
        "At first, it feels like big payments should be fraud, but that’s not true. Most fraud actually happens with smaller amounts. Surprisingly, very large transactions were usually genuine. So blocking only high-value payments would be a bad idea.\n",
        "\n",
        "**2) Risky Products:**\n",
        "Product C looks risky because it has the highest fraud rate (around 11%). Product W, even though it’s used the most, is generally safe and has much less fraud.\n",
        "\n",
        "**3) Card Types:**\n",
        "Fraud was seen more often with Discover cards compared to other card types. Also, credit cards had more fraud cases than debit cards.\n",
        "\n",
        "**4) Suspicious Emails:**\n",
        "Email domains like outlook.com and hotmail.com appeared more frequently in fraud transactions than other email domains.\n",
        "\n",
        "**5) Time of Day:**\n",
        "Most fraud activity happened early in the morning, especially between 7 AM and 9 AM. Normal users usually make transactions later in the day.\n",
        "\n",
        "**6) Missing Info:**\n",
        "Some columns, such as dist2 and several D features, have many missing values. We need to handle these carefully so they don’t negatively affect the model.\n",
        "\n",
        "**7) Best Clues:**\n",
        "Among all features, card3 showed a strong connection with fraud. Still, no single feature can detect fraud alone — combining multiple features works best."
